# Virtual environment — regenerates from uv.lock via `uv sync`
.venv/

# Python cache
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.pyo

# OS / OneDrive files
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini

# Secrets — never in git. `.env.example` IS committed, as the template.
#
# THE GLOB IS DELIBERATE, and it is a near-miss rather than tidiness. This read
# `.env` alone. On 2026-09-02, moving the API key out of the auto-loaded `.env`
# into an opt-in `.env.api`, `git check-ignore` reported `.env.api` — and
# `.env.local`, and `secrets/.env.api` — as TRACKED. Creating the file first and
# checking afterwards would have committed a live credential.
#
# So: every `.env*` is ignored, and the template is exempted back in. Adding a
# new secrets file must not depend on remembering to add a new ignore line.
.env*
!.env.example

# Claude Code persistent memory (local only)
memory/

# --- Generated pipeline artifacts ---
#
# Every pattern below is scoped to `companies/*/` so it applies to each company
# folder as it is added, not just to MORN. The reasoning attached to each pattern
# is unchanged from when it was written against the old top-level `data/` and
# `output/` layout; only the paths moved (Phase 2, 2026-08-12).
#
# companies/<TICKER>/data/raw/ is deliberately NOT ignored below in spirit, but it
# is large and re-fetchable. See DATA.md: the cache is precious locally (never
# re-hit EDGAR) but it is a *copy* of public EDGAR documents, so it does not
# belong in git.
companies/*/data/raw/

# Extracted sections regenerate from data/raw/ deterministically and for free.
companies/*/data/sections/

# WHERE A DETERMINISTIC STAGE'S CLOCK GOES.
#
# Five stages write committed artifacts that are pure functions of their inputs:
# discover, risk_diff, triage_8k, build_ledger and render_timeline. Each used to
# stamp the run's wall clock inside the artifact, which meant a re-run produced
# a diff in eight committed files with no change of substance — "run twice, diff
# nothing" (CLAUDE.md rule 4) could never hold. Harmless when stages were run by
# hand one at a time; structural once `pipeline` began touching all five on
# every run.
#
# The clock is real information, so it was moved rather than deleted. It lives
# here, where git does not watch it, and each artifact says so instead of
# carrying it.
#
# NOT ignored, and deliberately: `as_of_utc` and `index_fetched_utc` in
# inventory.json. Those describe when EDGAR was READ, not when the script ran —
# they are stable across re-runs and are the fields a coverage claim is checked
# against (VERIFICATION.md D9).
companies/*/data/_meta/

# Trimmed copies of the triage documents, held so the exact text sent to a model
# is an artifact on disk. Ignored for the same reason as data/raw/: it is a *copy*
# of public EDGAR documents, and it regenerates from them for free.
#
# The triage DECISIONS beside it are committed — see below.
companies/*/data/triage/text/

# companies/*/data/ledger/ IS COMMITTED. It was ignored on the same
# "it regenerates" reasoning as data/sections/, and for the ledger that reasoning
# is wrong:
#
#   - data/ledger/facts/ is model output. It cost $4.52 to produce and a re-run
#     would not reproduce it byte-for-byte, so it is the ONLY artifact in this
#     project that cannot be regenerated. It is also the audit record of what the
#     model actually returned, which is what makes every quote in the ledger
#     checkable after the fact.
#   - The rest of data/ledger/ derives from it deterministically, but derives
#     from nothing at all in a clean checkout, because data/raw/ and
#     data/sections/ are both ignored.
#
# 904 KB, and it is the analytical substance of the project. It belongs in git.

# companies/*/data/triage/triage-8k.json AND triage-report.md ARE COMMITTED
# (178 KB). They regenerate deterministically, so by the rule above they could be
# ignored — but they are the audit record config/forms.toml explicitly asks for
# ("Log every triage decision; a silent drop here loses real events"), and 15
# filings were dropped from the history on the strength of them. A record of what
# was excluded has to survive in the repository, or the exclusion is undocumented.

# companies/*/data/pack/ is ignored, and for MOST of what it holds
# "it regenerates" is actually true in the sense that matters: pack.json,
# index.json and timeline-events.json are a pure, deterministic function of
# data/ledger/, which IS committed. A clean checkout reproduces them
# byte-for-byte for free. Committing them would store a second copy of ~900 KB of
# the same content.
#
# What makes that safe is the sha256 of pack.json, recorded in pack-report.md and
# stamped onto every generated output. Without the hash, ignoring the pack would
# lose the answer to "which payload was this document written from"; with it, the
# answer is reproducible on demand.
#
# THE EXCEPTION, and the reason this is `companies/*/data/pack/*` with a negation
# rather than `companies/*/data/pack/`: gen-brief.json and gen-discussion.json are
# NOT derived from data/ledger/. They are model output — the raw response, the
# usage, and `text_before_repair`, which is the ONLY copy of each document as
# first written, before the citation and quotation repair rounds edited it. ~$3.50
# a pass, and no re-run reproduces them byte-for-byte. They were sitting in an
# ignored directory under a justification that describes the OTHER files beside
# them.
#
# This is the third time the "it regenerates" argument has been applied to model
# output in this repository. data/ledger/facts/ and output/ were both rescued from
# it (see the two blocks above); this one was not caught until the verification
# suite measured the consequence — tests/test_generate_outputs.py ran 29 checks in
# a clean checkout instead of 35 and reported green, because the six document-level
# checks quietly skip when these files are absent. That test now fails loudly
# instead. Fixed 2026-08-07, VERIFICATION.md D7.
companies/*/data/pack/*
!companies/*/data/pack/gen-*.json

# companies/*/output/ IS COMMITTED — the earlier "final deliverables regenerate
# from data/ledger/" reasoning was wrong for the same reason it was wrong for the
# ledger. The three deliverables are model-written prose. They cost real money to
# produce (~$3.50 a full pass), and a re-run will NOT reproduce them
# byte-for-byte, so "regenerable" is not true in the sense that matters. They are
# also the only artifact anyone outside this repo will actually read, and the thing
# every citation check runs against.
#
# Caught by the preflight check on 2026-08-06, before any output existed to lose.

# Per-run logs
logs/
*.log

# companies/*/output/pdf/ is ignored, and this is the data/pack/ case rather than
# the output/ case. The PDFs are a pure deterministic function of three committed
# things — the Markdown in companies/<TICKER>/output/, src/equity_research/render_pdf.py,
# and the [pdf] block in config/outputs.toml — and they cost $0.00 and about four
# seconds to rebuild:
#
#     uv run python -m equity_research.render_pdf
#
# Nothing in them is unrecoverable model output, unlike output/*.md or
# data/pack/gen-*.json, so the reasoning that rescued those does not apply here.
# Committing them would add a binary blob per document to the history on every
# regeneration, and a PDF is not byte-identical across runs anyway because it carries
# a creation timestamp.
#
# What makes that safe is that the render is verified: render_pdf.py reads each
# finished PDF back and fails if a single word, fact id, heading or at-risk glyph did
# not survive. A rebuilt PDF is checkably the same document, not merely assumed to be.
companies/*/output/pdf/
